{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"The opsadmin account on node1 is configured with the exact supplied SSH public key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin was created with the expected home and shell, and its authorized_keys file had restrictive ownership and permissions. After restart, the exact requested key text and its ED25519 fingerprint were directly observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-49aeddc2038b4b18","cmd-a91d1be9af8a484c"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"SSH authentication requires a public key rather than password or keyboard-interactive authentication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective sshd policy enabled public-key authentication, disabled password and keyboard-interactive authentication, and required AuthenticationMethods publickey. A password-path probe was rejected with Permission denied (publickey), both the policy and rejection persisted after restart, and sshd was active and listening.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a35ba231a3b54bfb","cmd-296328f600ba4e44","cmd-a91d1be9af8a484c"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"All observed mutations had clear purposes: cmd-49aeddc2038b4b18 created opsadmin and installed the requested key; cmd-a35ba231a3b54bfb created the required sshd policy and reloaded the service; cmd-77630a4bcfc845b0 scheduled the mandatory evidence restart. No unnecessary mutation was identified. After execution there were no failed services, package health remained sound, listening services were unchanged apart from post-restart PIDs, and temporary-file differences were normal boot-specific systemd directories.","evidence_ids":["cmd-49aeddc2038b4b18","cmd-a35ba231a3b54bfb","cmd-77630a4bcfc845b0","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, the restart was acknowledged, a transient connection timeout showed the disruption, the boot identity changed, and the system, sshd listener, account, exact key, and authentication policy recovered. The fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["No successful login using the corresponding private key was captured; access is supported by the exact installed public key, valid effective sshd configuration, active listener, and negative password-path tests rather than a positive key-authentication session.","The task-wide AuthenticationMethods publickey policy affects all SSH users, not only opsadmin; this is consistent with the instruction but broader than an account-specific restriction."]}
